CAT = "zapsiraPowers"; SEG = "POW"
# Criminal Procedure Act 51 of 1977 ss. 23, 42, 48, 49, 50 and Schedule 1 (Juta consolidation,
# justice.gov.za); Constitution ss. 12, 14, 35. A private security officer is not a peace officer.
Q = [
("A security officer arrests a suspect without a warrant. Under which law does that power normally come?",
 "The Criminal Procedure Act", ["The Firearms Control Act", "The Labour Relations Act", "The National Road Traffic Act"],
 "Arrest without a warrant by a private person, which includes a security officer, is governed by section 42 of the Criminal Procedure Act 51 of 1977."),
("In law, a private security officer making an arrest acts as which kind of person?",
 "A private person", ["A peace officer", "A police official", "A court sheriff"],
 "PSIRA registration does not make an officer a peace officer. A guard's arrest powers are those of any private person under section 42 of the Criminal Procedure Act."),
("Under section 42, a private person may arrest without a warrant someone who commits which offence in their presence?",
 "A Schedule 1 offence", ["Any municipal by-law breach", "Any traffic offence", "An unpaid civil debt"],
 "Section 42(1)(a) allows a private person to arrest someone who commits or attempts to commit a Schedule 1 offence in their presence, or whom they reasonably suspect of one."),
("Which of these is a Schedule 1 offence for which a guard may arrest a person caught in the act?",
 "Theft", ["Littering", "Jaywalking", "Not paying for parking"],
 "Schedule 1 to the Criminal Procedure Act includes theft, robbery, housebreaking with intent, fraud and malicious injury to property."),
("A guard sees two men fighting in the car park. Under section 42, what may the guard do?",
 "Arrest them for the affray", ["Nothing until police arrive", "Fine them on the spot", "Seize their car keys"],
 "Section 42(1)(d) allows a private person to arrest any person they see engaged in an affray (a fight in public)."),
("A guard authorised by a property owner finds a person stealing cables on that property. Which part of section 42 applies?",
 "The owner's authority", ["A peace officer's power", "The affray rule only", "No power applies here"],
 "Section 42(3) lets the owner, lawful occupier or person in charge of property, and anyone they authorise, arrest without a warrant a person found committing any offence on it."),
("After arresting a suspect, what must the guard do with the suspect?",
 "Hand over to SAPS promptly", ["Hold them overnight", "Release them after a warning", "Take them home to their family"],
 "Section 50 requires an arrested person to be brought to a police station as soon as possible. A guard calls SAPS and hands the suspect over without delay."),
("The Criminal Procedure Act allows an arrestor to use force only when the suspect does what?",
 "Resists or flees", ["Refuses to give a name", "Uses bad language", "Looks suspicious"],
 "Section 49(2) allows force only if the suspect resists the arrest or flees and cannot be arrested without force."),
("How much force may an arrestor use under section 49?",
 "Necessary and proportional", ["Any force the guard chooses", "Enough to punish the suspect", "Equal to the suspect's size"],
 "Section 49(2) limits force to what is reasonably necessary and proportional in the circumstances to overcome resistance or prevent flight."),
("A shoplifter caught with stolen soap pulls free and runs away. Under section 49, may the guard use deadly force?",
 "No, there is no threat", ["Yes, the suspect is fleeing", "Yes, if it is after dark", "Yes, if the item is valuable"],
 "Section 49(2) allows deadly force only if the suspect threatens serious violence or is suspected of a crime involving serious bodily harm and there is no other reasonable way to arrest."),
("When does section 49 allow deadly force during an arrest?",
 "Threat of serious violence", ["Theft of expensive goods", "Refusal to stop when told", "Damage to a client's vehicle"],
 "Deadly force is lawful only where the suspect poses a threat of serious violence to the arrestor or another person, or the second ground in section 49(2)(b) applies."),
("A guard arrests a suspect who is carrying a knife. What may the guard do with the knife?",
 "Put it in safe custody", ["Keep it as a trophy", "Throw it into a drain", "Hand it back to the suspect"],
 "Section 23(2) lets the person making an arrest place in safe custody any object that could be used to cause bodily harm. It is then handed to SAPS."),
("A guard who is not a peace officer arrests a thief holding a stolen phone. What does section 23 allow the guard to do with the phone?",
 "Seize it and give it to SAPS", ["Search the suspect's home for more", "Return it to the shop's shelf", "Keep it for the client's insurance"],
 "Section 23(1)(b) lets a private arrestor seize such an article and requires it to be delivered to a police official forthwith."),
("When making an arrest, what should the guard tell the suspect?",
 "The arrest and its reason", ["Nothing until police arrive", "That they will be fined", "That they must confess now"],
 "The suspect must know they are being arrested and the reason. Section 35 of the Constitution also gives arrested persons the right to be told promptly of their right to remain silent."),
("Which right does section 35 of the Constitution give to every arrested person?",
 "To remain silent", ["To be released at once", "To choose the arresting officer", "To leave if they apologise"],
 "Section 35(1) gives everyone arrested for an alleged offence the right to remain silent and to be informed promptly of that right."),
("Within how long must an arrested person normally be brought before a court?",
 "48 hours", ["12 hours", "24 hours", "72 hours"],
 "Section 35(1)(d) of the Constitution and section 50 of the Criminal Procedure Act set 48 hours. That is the police's duty after the guard hands over."),
("A visitor refuses a bag search at the mall entrance where search on entry is a posted condition. What may the guard lawfully do?",
 "Refuse entry", ["Search the bag by force", "Arrest the visitor", "Confiscate the bag"],
 "A search at the entrance is a condition of entry the visitor may decline. Without consent or a lawful arrest the guard may not search by force, but may refuse entry."),
("A guard suspects a person of theft but has no reasonable grounds. Can the guard lawfully arrest?",
 "No, grounds are needed", ["Yes, any suspicion is enough", "Yes, if the client agrees", "Yes, if it is done quickly"],
 "Section 42 requires a reasonable suspicion based on facts. An arrest on a hunch is unlawful and can expose the officer and employer to a damages claim."),
("Which right does section 12 of the Constitution protect that limits how long a guard may hold someone?",
 "Freedom and security", ["Freedom of expression", "Access to information", "Freedom of trade"],
 "Section 12 protects the right not to be deprived of freedom arbitrarily or without just cause. Holding someone without a lawful arrest can amount to unlawful detention."),
("A guard locks a suspected shoplifter in a storeroom for three hours to make him confess. What is this?",
 "Unlawful detention", ["A lawful citizen's arrest", "Standard loss control", "A reasonable precaution"],
 "Holding someone to extract a confession is not a lawful arrest. An arrested person must be handed to SAPS as soon as possible."),
("A guard sees someone stealing a car radio and chases the thief off the client's property. Which rule allows the chase?",
 "Fresh pursuit in section 42", ["The general duty to patrol", "There is no such rule", "Only peace officers may chase"],
 "Section 42(2) lets a private person who may arrest under section 42(1)(a) pursue the suspect at once, and others told of the purpose may help."),
("Before breaking into premises to arrest a suspect hiding inside, what does section 48 require?",
 "Audibly demand entry", ["A client's written order", "Waiting until morning", "A neighbour's permission"],
 "Section 48 requires the person to audibly demand entry and state the purpose, and to be refused, before breaking open premises to make a lawful arrest."),
("A guard handcuffs a calm suspect who is cooperating fully. What could make this unlawful?",
 "Force beyond the need", ["The suspect's clothing", "The time of the arrest", "The colour of the cuffs"],
 "Any restraint is force. Where it is not needed to prevent escape or harm, it may be more than reasonably necessary under section 49."),
("A guard arrests a suspect and slaps him for swearing. What is the legal position?",
 "It is assault", ["It is reasonable force", "It is allowed after arrest", "It is a lawful warning"],
 "Force after the suspect is under control serves no lawful purpose. Hitting a detained person is assault."),
("A person loiters at the estate gate and refuses to leave, but commits no offence. What is the guard's best action?",
 "Observe, record, call SAPS", ["Arrest them for loitering", "Push them down the road", "Take their ID book away"],
 "Without an offence there are no arrest grounds. The guard keeps watch, records details and calls SAPS if the person becomes a threat."),
("Who decides whether an arrested shoplifter will be charged?",
 "SAPS and the prosecutor", ["The security officer", "The shop's cashier", "The security company owner"],
 "After the hand-over, SAPS investigates and the National Prosecuting Authority decides on prosecution. The guard's role is to give a statement as a witness."),
("A guard thinks a vehicle leaving the site holds stolen goods but the driver refuses a search. What should the guard do?",
 "Note details, inform SAPS", ["Puncture the tyres", "Smash a window to look", "Block the driver in for hours"],
 "Without consent or a lawful arrest, a guard may not force a search. Recording the registration, description and direction and calling SAPS is the lawful route."),
("A guard must physically restrain a suspect who resists arrest. When must the force stop?",
 "Once the resistance ends", ["When police arrive only", "After a warning blow", "When the suspect apologises"],
 "Force is lawful only while it is needed to overcome resistance or prevent flight. Once the suspect is under control, it must stop."),
]
